## 15. INTER-STATE RELATIONS

**[SYL: GS2-B.1] | [LAXMIKANTH: Chapter 15] | [BARE ACT: Arts. 262, 263, 261, 301-307]**

### 15.1. Introduction

The successful functioning of the Indian federal system depends not only on harmonious relations and close cooperation between the Centre and States, but also between the States inter se. The Constitution makes provisions for inter-State comity through:

1. Adjudication of inter-State water disputes (Art. 262)
2. Coordination through inter-State Councils (Art. 263)
3. Mutual recognition of public acts, records and judicial proceedings (Art. 261)
4. Freedom of inter-State trade, commerce and intercourse (Arts. 301-307)
5. Zonal Councils (established by States Reorganisation Act, 1956) to promote inter-State cooperation

### 15.2. INTER-STATE WATER DISPUTES (ARTICLE 262)

Article 262 provides for adjudication of inter-State water disputes. It makes two provisions:

- **Parliamentary power**: Parliament may by law provide for adjudication of any dispute or complaint with respect to use, distribution and control of waters of any inter-State river and river valley.
- **Exclusion of jurisdiction**: Parliament may provide that neither SC nor any other court shall exercise jurisdiction in respect of any such dispute/complaint.

#### 15.2.1. Laws Enacted

| Law | Provision |
|---|---|
| **River Boards Act, 1956** | Provides for establishment of River Boards by Central Govt on request of State Govts to advise on regulation and development of inter-State river/valleys. |
| **Inter-State Water Disputes Act, 1956** | Empowers Central Govt to set up ad hoc tribunals for adjudication of disputes between States re: waters of inter-State rivers/valleys. Tribunal's decision is final and binding. SC and other courts have no jurisdiction. |

#### 15.2.2. Inter-State Water Dispute Tribunals Constituted So Far

| S.No. | Tribunal | Year Constituted | States Involved |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1. | Krishna Water Disputes Tribunal-I | 1969 | Maharashtra, Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh |
| 2. | Godavari Water Disputes Tribunal | 1969 | Maharashtra, Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh, Madhya Pradesh, Odisha |
| 3. | Narmada Water Disputes Tribunal | 1969 | Rajasthan, Gujarat, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra |
| 4. | Ravi and Beas Water Disputes Tribunal | 1986 | Punjab, Haryana, Rajasthan |
| 5. | Cauvery Water Disputes Tribunal | 1990 | Karnataka, Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Puducherry |
| 6. | Krishna Water Disputes Tribunal-II | 2004 | Maharashtra, Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh |
| 7. | Vansadhara Water Disputes Tribunal | 2010 | Odisha and Andhra Pradesh |
| 8. | Mahadayi Water Disputes Tribunal | 2010 | Goa, Karnataka, Maharashtra |
| 9. | Mahanadi Water Disputes Tribunal | 2018 | Odisha and Chhattisgarh |

*Note:* Adjudication by tribunals is preferred over courts as water disputes involve public interest, not just private proprietary rights.

### 15.3. INTER-STATE COUNCILS (ARTICLE 263)

Article 263 empowers the President to establish an Inter-State Council if it appears that public interest would be served. President can define its duties, organisation and procedure.

#### 15.3.1. Duties of Inter-State Council (as per Art. 263)

(a) Enquire into and advise upon disputes which may arise between States;
(b) Investigate and discuss subjects in which States or Centre-States have a common interest;
(c) Make recommendations upon any such subject, particularly for better coordination of policy and action on it.

*Note:* Council's role is advisory (unlike SC's binding decisions under Art. 131). It can deal with legal or non-legal controversies.

#### 15.3.2. Councils Established Under Article 263

- Central Council of Health and Family Welfare
- Central Council of Local Government (earlier Central Council of Local Self-Government)
- Four Regional Councils for Sales Tax (Northern, Eastern, Western, Southern Zones)

#### 15.3.3. Permanent Inter-State Council (1990)

On recommendation of Sarkaria Commission (1983-88), Janata Dal Govt under V.P. Singh established a permanent Inter-State Council in 1990.

**Composition:**
- Prime Minister - Chairman
- Chief Ministers of all States
- Chief Ministers of UTs with legislative assemblies
- Administrators of UTs without legislative assemblies
- Governors of States under President's Rule
- 6 Central Cabinet Ministers (including Home Minister) nominated by PM
- 5 Ministers of Cabinet rank/Minister of State (independent charge) as permanent invitees (nominated by Chairman)

**Functions:**
- Investigate and discuss subjects of common interest to States/Centre
- Make recommendations for better coordination of policy and action
- Deliberate on matters of general interest to States referred by Chairman

**Meetings:** At least thrice a year; held in camera; decisions by consensus.

**Standing Committee (set up 1996):** Chairman - Union Home Minister, members - 5 Union Cabinet Ministers + 9 Chief Ministers. For continuous consultation and processing of matters.

**Secretariat:** Inter-State Council Secretariat set up in 1991, headed by Secretary to GoI. Since 2011, also functions as Secretariat of Zonal Councils.

### 15.4. PUBLIC ACTS, RECORDS AND JUDICIAL PROCEEDINGS (ARTICLE 261)

To ensure mutual recognition across States:

- **Full faith and credit**: Throughout territory of India, full faith and credit shall be given to public acts, records and judicial proceedings of Centre and every State. 'Public acts' include legislative and executive acts; 'public record' includes official books/registers/records made by public servant in official duty.
- **Parliamentary regulation**: Manner of proof and effect as provided by Parliament.
- **Execution of civil judgements**: Final judgements/orders of civil courts in any part of India are capable of execution anywhere in India without fresh suit. Does **not** apply to criminal judgements/penal laws of other States.

### 15.5. INTER-STATE TRADE AND COMMERCE (ARTICLES 301-307)

#### 15.5.1. Freedom of Trade, Commerce and Intercourse (Art. 301)

Article 301 declares trade, commerce and intercourse throughout territory of India shall be **free**. Applies to both inter-State and intra-State trade/commerce/intercourse. Freedom is from all restrictions except those provided in Arts. 302-305.

#### 15.5.2. Restrictions on Freedom (Arts. 302-305)

| Provision | Power | Conditions/Limitations |
|---|---|---|
| **Art. 302** | Parliament can impose restrictions on freedom of trade/commerce/intercourse between States or within State in **public interest**. | Cannot give preference to one State over another or discriminate between States except in case of **scarcity of goods** in any part of India. |
| **Art. 303** | Prohibits Parliament/State Legislatures from giving preference or discriminating between States by virtue of any entry relating to trade/commerce. | Exceptions as per Art. 302 and Art. 304(b). |
| **Art. 304** | **(a)** State can impose on goods imported from other States/UTs any tax to which similar goods manufactured in that State are subject (prohibits discriminatory taxation). **(b)** State can impose reasonable restrictions on freedom of trade/commerce/intercourse with or within that State in **public interest**. | Bill for (b) requires **previous sanction of President**. Cannot discriminate between States. |
| **Art. 305** | Saving of existing laws and laws providing for **State monopolies** (nationalisation). | Parliament/State Legislature can create monopolies in favour of Centre/State for any trade/business/industry/service, wholly or partially. |

**Art. 306** was repealed by 7th Amendment Act, 1956.
**Art. 307** empowers Parliament to appoint appropriate authority to carry out purposes of Arts. 301-304 (no authority appointed so far).

### 15.6. ZONAL COUNCILS

Zonal Councils are **statutory** bodies established by **States Reorganisation Act, 1956** (not constitutional). Country divided into five zones initially; North-Eastern Council created separately later.

#### 15.6.1. Composition

- **Chairman**: Union Home Minister (common for all five Zonal Councils)
- **Vice-Chairman**: Chief Minister of each State in rotation (one year)
- **Members**: Chief Ministers and 2 other Ministers from each State in the zone; Administrator of each UT in the zone
- **Advisors (without voting rights)**: Person nominated by Planning Commission (now NITI Aayog context), Chief Secretaries, Development Commissioners of States in zone

#### 15.6.2. Objectives/Functions

- Achieve emotional integration of the country
- Arrest growth of acute state-consciousness, regionalism, linguism, particularistic trends
- Help remove after-effects of separation/reorganisation
- Centre-State cooperation in social/economic matters; exchange of ideas/experience for uniform policies
- Cooperation in execution of major development projects
- Secure political equilibrium between different regions

Zonal Councils are **deliberative and advisory** bodies only.

#### 15.6.3. Zonal Councils at a Glance

| Council | States/UTs Covered | Headquarters |
|---|---|---|
| **Northern Zonal Council** | Himachal Pradesh, Haryana, Punjab, Rajasthan, Delhi, Chandigarh, J&K, Ladakh | New Delhi |
| **Central Zonal Council** | Uttar Pradesh, Uttarakhand, Chhattisgarh, Madhya Pradesh | Prayagraj (Allahabad) |
| **Eastern Zonal Council** | Bihar, Jharkhand, West Bengal, Odisha | Kolkata |
| **Western Zonal Council** | Gujarat, Maharashtra, Goa, Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu | Mumbai |
| **Southern Zonal Council** | Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, Kerala, Puducherry | Chennai |

#### 15.6.4. North-Eastern Council (NEC)

Created by **North-Eastern Council Act, 1971** (separate from Zonal Councils). Members: Assam, Manipur, Mizoram, Arunachal Pradesh, Nagaland, Meghalaya, Tripura, Sikkim (added in 2002). Functions similar to Zonal Councils with additions - formulate unified/coordinated regional plan for matters of common importance; review measures for maintenance of security/public order in region.

### 15.7. PYQs (PRELIMS + MAINS) - LAST 10 YEARS

| Year | Exam | Question | Topic | Approach |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2016 | Prelims | Inter-State Council is mentioned in which Article? | Art. 263 | Art. 263, established in 1990 on Sarkaria recommendation |
| 2017 | Mains | Inter-State water disputes mechanism in India | Art. 262, Tribunals | River Boards Act 1956, Inter-State Water Disputes Act 1956, tribunals |
| 2018 | Prelims | Zonal Councils are constitutional or statutory? | Zonal Councils | Statutory bodies under States Reorganisation Act, 1956 |
| 2019 | Prelims | Article 301 deals with? | Inter-State Trade & Commerce | Freedom of trade, commerce and intercourse |
| 2020 | Mains | Role of Inter-State Council in promoting cooperative federalism | Art. 263 | Advisory body, consensus-based, promotes coordination |

### 15.8. CURRENT AFFAIRS LINKAGE (2023-2026)

| Issue | Linkage | Mains Angle |
|---|---|---|
| Inter-State water disputes (Cauvery, Krishna, Mahadayi) | Art. 262, Tribunals | Delayed awards, enforcement issues, need for permanent mechanism |
| Inter-State Council meetings & discussions on cooperative federalism | Art. 263 | Platform for Centre-State coordination, GST Council complementarity |
| Free movement of goods & services post-GST | Art. 301, GST regime | Impact on internal trade barriers, 'One Nation One Market' objective |

