## 16. EMERGENCY PROVISIONS

**[SYL: GS2-B.2] | [LAXMIKANTH: Chapter 16] | [BARE ACT: Part XVIII (Arts. 352-360)]**

### 16.1. Introduction

Emergency provisions (Part XVIII, Arts. 352-360) enable Central Govt to meet abnormal situations. They allow conversion of federal structure into unitary structure without formal constitutional amendment during Emergency. Dr. B.R. Ambedkar described Constitution as 'unitary as well as federal according to requirements of time and circumstances' - federal in normal times, unitary during emergencies.

**Three types of Emergencies:**
1. **National Emergency** (Art. 352) - War, external aggression or armed rebellion
2. **President's Rule/State Emergency/Constitutional Emergency** (Art. 356) - Failure of constitutional machinery in a State
3. **Financial Emergency** (Art. 360) - Threat to financial stability/credit of India or any part of its territory

### 16.2. NATIONAL EMERGENCY (ARTICLE 352)

#### 16.2.1. Grounds for Proclamation

President can proclaim National Emergency when security of India or any part thereof is threatened by:
- **War**
- **External aggression**
- **Armed rebellion** (substituted for 'internal disturbance' by 44th Amendment Act, 1978)

Emergency can be proclaimed even before actual occurrence if there is **imminent danger**. Can be limited to a part of India (42nd Amendment Act, 1976). Different proclamations possible on different grounds even if one is in operation.

**External Emergency** = declared on grounds of war/external aggression; **Internal Emergency** = declared on ground of armed rebellion.

#### 16.2.2. Parliamentary Approval and Duration

- **Approval required:** Within **1 month** from date of issue by both Houses of Parliament (reduced from 2 months by 44th Amendment). If Lok Sabha dissolved/non-reconstituted, survives till 30 days from first sitting of new Lok Sabha provided Rajya Sabha approved in meantime.
- **Continued operation:** If approved, continues for **6 months**. Can be extended indefinitely with Parliamentary approval for every **6 months**.
- **Special majority:** Resolution approving proclamation/continuance requires majority of total membership of House + majority of not less than **2/3 of members present and voting** (44th Amendment).
- **Cabinet recommendation mandatory:** Proclamation must be based on written recommendation of **Cabinet** (44th Amendment) - prevents PM from acting alone.
- **Judicial review:** Declaration is justiciable on grounds of malafide or extraneous/irrelevant facts (*Minerva Mills* case, 1980).

#### 16.2.3. Revocation

- President can revoke by subsequent proclamation (no Parliamentary approval needed).
- **Lok Sabha disapproval:** If Lok Sabha passes resolution disapproving continuation (by simple majority), President must revoke. Also, **1/10th members of Lok Sabha** can give written notice to Speaker (or President if House not in session) requiring special sitting within **14 days** to consider disapproval resolution.

#### 16.2.4. Effects of National Emergency

**(A) Centre-State Relations**
- **Executive:** Centre can give directions to States on any matter
- **Legislative:** Parliament empowered to legislate on **State List** subjects (Art. 250). Laws remain in force even after emergency ends
- **Financial:** President can modify financial relations between Centre and States (Art. 354)

**(B) Effect on Lok Sabha and State Assemblies**
- Life of Lok Sabha may be extended by **1 year at a time** (not beyond 6 months after emergency ceases) by law of Parliament
- Life of State Assemblies may be similarly extended

**(C) Effect on Fundamental Rights**
- **Art. 358 (Automatic suspension):** On proclamation of National Emergency **on ground of war/external aggression only**, **Art. 19** is automatically suspended. During internal emergency (armed rebellion), Art. 19 is **NOT** automatically suspended.
- **Art. 359 (Suspension of enforcement):** President may by order suspend enforcement of **any** Fundamental Rights except **Arts. 20 and 21** (44th Amendment). Applies to both external and internal emergencies. Only laws related to emergency and actions taken thereunder are protected; unrelated laws not protected. Protection ends with order ceasing to operate (but actions during operation remain immune).

**Distinction Arts. 358 vs 359:** Art. 358 applies only to Art. 19 and only during external emergency; automatic. Art. 359 can cover other FRs (except 20,21), applies to both types, not automatic (needs Presidential Order).

**Declarations so far:** 1962 (China - external), 1971 (Pakistan - external), 1975 (internal disturbance/armed rebellion context) - revoked in 1977. 1975 Emergency most controversial; led to 44th Amendment safeguards and Shah Commission.

### 16.3. PRESIDENT'S RULE (ARTICLE 356) - CONSTITUTIONAL EMERGENCY

#### 16.3.1. Grounds of Imposition

President's Rule can be proclaimed if satisfied that government of a State cannot be carried on in accordance with Constitution:
- **On Governor's report** or **otherwise** (even without report)
- **Art. 365:** Failure of State to comply with/ give effect to Centre's directions → President can hold constitutional machinery has failed

#### 16.3.2. Parliamentary Approval and Duration

- **Approval:** Within **2 months** by both Houses (simple majority). If Lok Sabha dissolved, survives till 30 days from first sitting of new Lok Sabha provided Rajya Sabha approved.
- **Duration:** Initially 6 months; extendable by **6 months at a time** up to maximum **3 years** total.
- **Restriction beyond 1 year (44th Amendment):** Beyond 1 year, extension allowed only if (a) **National Emergency** is in operation in whole or part of State, AND (b) **Election Commission** certifies elections to State Assembly cannot be held due to difficulties.
- **Revocation:** By President's proclamation anytime (no Parliamentary approval). No provision for Lok Sabha to pass disapproval resolution (unlike National Emergency).

#### 16.3.3. Consequences of President's Rule

- **Ministry dismissed**, Governor administers State on behalf of President (with Chief Secretary/advisors)
- **State Legislature suspended or dissolved**. Parliament legislates for State.
- **Parliamentary delegation:** Parliament may delegate law-making power to President or specified authority (President's Acts in practice, made in consultation with MPs from that State)
- **Ordinances:** President can promulgate ordinances when Parliament not in session
- **Expenditure:** President can authorise expenditure from State Consolidated Fund when Lok Sabha not in session pending Parliamentary sanction
- **High Court unaffected:** President cannot assume powers of State High Court or suspend provisions relating to it.

#### 16.3.4. Judicial Review & Safeguards (Bommai Case, 1994)

**Landmark principles from** ***S.R. Bommai v. Union of India* (1994):**
- Presidential proclamation under Art. 356 is **subject to judicial review**
- Satisfaction must be based on **relevant material**; action can be struck down if based on irrelevant/extraneous/malafide/perverse grounds
- Burden of proof on Centre to show relevant material exists
- Court can review relevance but not adequacy of material
- If proclamation unconstitutional, court can restore dismissed government and revive assembly if dissolved/suspended
- Assembly should be **dissolved only after Parliament approves** proclamation; until approval, only suspension possible. If Parliament disapproves, assembly reactivated
- **Secularism** is a **basic feature** of Constitution - govt pursuing anti-secular politics liable to action under Art. 356
- Question of loss of majority must be decided on **floor of House**, not by Governor's subjective assessment
- New party at Centre cannot dismiss State ministries formed by other parties for that reason
- Art. 356 is **exceptional power** to be used only occasionally

**Proper use (as per Bommai):** Hung assembly with no party able to form govt; ministry resigns/defeated with no alternative; disregard of Central Constitutional directions; internal subversion/physical breakdown endangering security.

**Improper use:** Dismissing ministry without probing alternative; Governor assessing majority without floor test; punishment for electoral defeat at Centre; maladministration/corruption alone (without subversion); use for intraparty purposes/extraneous reasons.

**Use:** Imposed over 125 times since 1950 - often misused for political purposes. First in Punjab 1951.

### 16.4. FINANCIAL EMERGENCY (ARTICLE 360)

#### 16.4.1. Grounds, Approval, Duration

- **Grounds:** President satisfied that financial stability or credit of India or any part thereof is **threatened**
- **Approval:** Within **2 months** by both Houses (simple majority). Similar dissolution contingency as Art. 356.
- **Duration:** If approved, continues **indefinitely** till revoked (no maximum period, no requirement of periodic approval every 6 months)
- **Revocation:** By President anytime by subsequent proclamation (no Parliamentary approval)
- **Judicial review:** Satisfaction not beyond judicial review (44th Amendment removed 38th Amendment bar)

#### 16.4.2. Effects of Financial Emergency

- **Centre-State financial control:** Centre can direct States to observe **canons of financial propriety** and issue other necessary directions
- **Financial Bills:** President may require States to **reserve** Money Bills/other financial Bills for his consideration
- **Salary reduction:** President can direct reduction of salaries/allowances of persons serving Union (including **judges of SC and HCs**)
- **State salary reduction:** Directions can require reduction of salaries/allowances of State Govt employees

**Note:** No Financial Emergency has been proclaimed so far (though crisis in 1991).

### 16.5. COMPARATIVE ANALYSIS

| Aspect | National Emergency (Art. 352) | President's Rule (Art. 356) | Financial Emergency (Art. 360) |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Grounds** | War/external aggression/armed rebellion | Failure of constitutional machinery in State | Threat to financial stability/credit of India or part |
| **Scope** | Whole or part of India | Specific State(s) | Whole or part of India |
| **Effect on FRs** | Art. 19 suspended automatically only in external emergency; others (except 20,21) can be suspended by order | No effect on FRs | No direct suspension of FRs |
| **State Legislature** | Continues (may be extended) | Suspended/dissolved; Parliament legislates | Continues |
| **Parliamentary Approval** | Both Houses, special majority, within 1 month, every 6 months if extended | Both Houses, simple majority, within 2 months, every 6 months up to 3 years (with conditions beyond 1 year) | Both Houses, simple majority, within 2 months, indefinite once approved |
| **Revocation** | By President or by Lok Sabha resolution (simple majority) | By President only | By President only |
| **Centre-State** | Centre gets powers re: State List; financial relations modified | State comes under direct Centre control | Centre controls financial matters of States |

### 16.6. CRITICISM & SAFEGUARDS

**Criticism:** Destruction of federalism, concentration of power in Centre/Executive, President could become dictator, threat to State autonomy, FRs become meaningless, misuse for political purposes.

**Safeguards introduced by 44th Amendment Act, 1978:**
- 'Internal disturbance' replaced by 'armed rebellion'
- Cabinet recommendation mandatory for National Emergency
- Judicial review of proclamations restored
- Art. 19 suspension only in external emergency (automatic); Arts. 20,21 never suspendable
- Lok Sabha can disapprove National Emergency
- Special majority for approval of National Emergency
- Restrictions on extending President's Rule beyond 1 year
- President's satisfaction not final/conclusive
- Revocation easier with Parliamentary control

