## 19. PRIME MINISTER

**[SYL: GS2-C.1] | [LAXMIKANTH: Chapter 19] | [BARE ACT: Arts. 74-75, 78]**

### 19.1. Introduction

Prime Minister is the **real executive** authority in India (head of government), while President is nominal head of state. PM is leader of Council of Ministers, leader of House of Parliament (usually Lok Sabha), and chief spokesperson of government. In parliamentary system, PM is keystone of cabinet arch.

### 19.2. APPOINTMENT OF PRIME MINISTER (Art. 75)

Appointed by **President**. Constitution doesn't specify procedure - governed by conventions.

**Conventions:**
- **Single party majority:** Leader of party with majority in Lok Sabha appointed as PM
- **No single party majority (Hung Parliament):** President uses discretion - usually invites leader of largest party/coalition who can command majority. May ask to prove majority on floor of House
- **PM from Rajya Sabha:** Possible if enjoys confidence of Lok Sabha (e.g., Indira Gandhi 1966, Deve Gowda 1996, Manmohan Singh 2004)

**Appointment of Ministers:** Done by President on **advice of PM** (Art. 75(1)).

### 19.3. OATH, TERM, SALARY

**Oath (Art. 75(4)):** Administered by President. Oath of office and oath of secrecy.
- **Oath of office:** Faithful discharge of duties, uphold sovereignty/integrity of India, preserve/protect/defend Constitution
- **Oath of secrecy:** Not directly or indirectly communicate any matter to unauthorized person

**Term:** No fixed term - holds office during **pleasure of President**. However, this means as long as PM enjoys majority support in **Lok Sabha**. If loses confidence, must resign.

**Salary:** Determined by Parliament; also gets sumptuary allowance etc.

### 19.4. POWERS AND FUNCTIONS OF PRIME MINISTER

#### 19.4.1. In Relation to Council of Ministers
- Recommends persons who can be appointed as ministers by President
- Allocates portfolios among ministers
- Can reshuffle/reorganise Council of Ministers
- Can ask a minister to resign; can advise President to dismiss minister
- Presides over meetings of Cabinet and Council of Ministers
- Guides, directs, controls and coordinates activities of all ministers
- Can bring about collapse of Council of Ministers by resigning (or by advice to dissolve Lok Sabha in certain situations)

#### 19.4.2. In Relation to President
- Principal channel of communication between President and Council of Ministers (Art. 78)
- Advises President re: appointment of important officials (CAG, CEC, ECs, UPSC Chairman/Members, Governors, Attorney General)
- Advises President re: summoning/proroguing Parliament, dissolving Lok Sabha
- Advises President re: proclamation of emergencies
- Furnishes information to President as required by Art. 78

#### 19.4.3. In Relation to Parliament
- Leader of House (usually Lok Sabha) - advises President on summoning/proroguing/dissolving
- Announces major policies on floor of House
- Represents government in Parliament, answers questions
- Pilots all important bills
- Can advise President for dissolution of Lok Sabha

#### 19.4.4. Other Powers
- Chairman of **NITI Aayog** (replaced Planning Commission)
- Chairman of **Cabinet Committee on Security**, **Appointments Committee of Cabinet**, etc.
- Chief spokesman of Union Government
- Crisis manager-in-chief during emergencies
- Link between government and people
- Leader of nation in international forums

### 19.5. ROLE OF PRIME MINISTER

**Bagehot's description:** 'Keystone of the cabinet arch' - central figure.
**Ivor Jennings:** Emphasised cabinet system; later analysts noted shift to **prime ministerial government** (Crossman, Mackintosh) - PM became dominant, especially with strong majority.

**In India:** PM's role has grown significantly. With one-party majority and disciplined party, PM can be very powerful. With coalition, need to accommodate coalition partners.

### 19.6. PM AND COUNCIL OF MINISTERS - RELATIONSHIP

Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to **Lok Sabha** (Art. 75). PM is head of Council of Ministers - coordinates and directs. Ministers are appointed on PM's advice and hold office during President's pleasure (on PM's advice).

### 19.7. PYQs (PRELIMS + MAINS)

| Year | Exam | Question | Topic | Approach |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2016 | Prelims | Prime Minister can be member of which House? | Appointment | Either House of Parliament (Art. 75) |
| 2017 | Mains | Prime Ministerial government vs cabinet government in India | Role | Increasing dominance of PM, especially in majority governments |
| 2018 | Prelims | Who advises President on dissolution of Lok Sabha? | Powers | Prime Minister |
| 2019 | Mains | Role of PM in parliamentary democracy | Functions | Link between legislature/executive, policy leadership, crisis management |

### 19.8. MIND MAP

```text
PRIME MINISTER (ARTS. 74-75, 78)
├── APPOINTMENT
│   ├── By President; leader of majority party in Lok Sabha
│   ├── Can be from Rajya Sabha if commands Lok Sabha confidence
│   └── President uses discretion in hung Parliament
├── POWERS
│   ├── Council of Ministers (formation, reshuffle, removal of ministers)
│   ├── President (advice on appointments, emergencies, dissolution)
│   ├── Parliament (leader of House, policy announcements)
│   └── NITI Aayog Chairman, key Cabinet Committees
├── ROLE
│   ├── Real executive, head of government
│   ├── 'Keystone of cabinet arch'
│   └── Shift to prime ministerial government in practice
└── RESPONSIBILITY
    ├── Council collectively responsible to Lok Sabha (Art. 75)
    └── Individual responsibility of ministers
```

