## 22. PARLIAMENT

**[SYL: GS2-C.2] | [LAXMIKANTH: Chapter 22] | [BARE ACT: Arts. 79-122]**

### 22.1. Introduction

Parliament is the **legislature** of Union. Article 79: Parliament consists of President, Council of States (Rajya Sabha), House of People (Lok Sabha). Bicameral legislature.

### 22.2. COMPOSITION

**Rajya Sabha (Council of States):** Permanent House, not subject to dissolution. Max strength 250 (238 elected by States/UTs, 12 nominated by President). Currently 245. Represents States, ensures federal character.

**Lok Sabha (House of People):** Lower House, subject to dissolution. Max strength 552 (530 from States, 20 from UTs, 2 nominated from Anglo-Indian community - Anglo-Indian nomination discontinued by 104th Amendment Act, 2019 w.e.f. 25 Jan 2020). Directly elected by people on basis of adult franchise.

### 22.3. SYSTEM OF ELECTIONS

**Lok Sabha:** First-past-the-post (simple majority) system. By **single-member constituencies**.
**Rajya Sabha:** Elected by **proportional representation by single transferable vote** by members of State Legislative Assemblies. UTs of Delhi and Puducherry also elect members to Rajya Sabha.

### 22.4. DURATION

**Lok Sabha:** Normal term **5 years** from first meeting; can be dissolved earlier. During **National Emergency**, term can be extended by **1 year at a time** (not beyond 6 months after emergency ends).
**Rajya Sabha:** **Permanent House** - not dissolved. 1/3 members retire every **2 years**, so each member has term of **6 years**.

### 22.5. PRESIDING OFFICERS

**Lok Sabha:** Speaker (elected by members), Deputy Speaker. Speaker presides, has casting vote, can suspend/disqualify members, decides on money bills (certificate under Art. 110).
**Rajya Sabha:** Chairman is **Vice-President of India** (ex-officio), Deputy Chairman elected by members. When VP acts as President, Deputy Chairman presides.

### 22.6. SESSIONS, DEVICES, LEGISLATIVE PROCEDURE

**Sessions:** At least **two sessions** per year, not more than **6 months** gap between two sessions. (Usually Budget Session, Monsoon Session, Winter Session)
**Quorum:** 1/10th of total membership.

**Parliamentary Devices to control Executive:** Question Hour, Zero Hour, Adjournment Motion, No-Confidence Motion, Censure Motion, Calling Attention Motion, Short Duration Discussion, Motion of Thanks, Resolutions etc.

**Legislative Procedure:** Ordinary Bills can originate in either House (except Money Bills in Lok Sabha only). Money Bills (Art. 110) - introduced only in Lok Sabha with President's recommendation, Rajya Sabha can suggest amendments but Lok Sabha may accept/reject; Rajya Sabha must return within 14 days, else deemed passed.

**Joint Sitting (Art. 108):** President can summon joint sitting to resolve deadlock on Ordinary Bill (not Money Bill/Constitutional Amendment Bill), presided over by **Speaker of Lok Sabha**.

### 22.7. BUDGET (ANNUAL FINANCIAL STATEMENT - ART. 112)

Presented by Finance Minister in Lok Sabha. Stages include presentation, general discussion, voting on demands for grants, appropriation bill, finance bill. **Cut motions** allow members to oppose policies.

### 22.8. PARLIAMENTARY PRIVILEGES (ARTS. 105, 194)

Rights and immunities to enable Houses to function effectively. Include freedom of speech, right to publish proceedings, exemption from arrest in civil cases during session, right to exclude strangers, power to punish for breach of privilege/contempt.

### 22.9. SOVEREIGNTY OF PARLIAMENT

Indian Parliament is **not sovereign** like British Parliament - it is subject to Constitution. Limited by **Fundamental Rights**, **Judicial Review**, **federalism**, and **Basic Structure** doctrine (*Kesavananda Bharati* case).

### 22.10. PYQs

| Year | Exam | Question | Topic | Approach |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2016 | Prelims | Rajya Sabha is a permanent House because? | Rajya Sabha | Not subject to dissolution, 1/3 retire every 2 years |
| 2017 | Mains | Money Bill - procedure and Rajya Sabha's role | Money Bill | Art. 110, introduced only in LS, RS limited role |
| 2018 | Prelims | Joint sitting of Parliament is presided over by? | Joint Sitting | Speaker of Lok Sabha |
| 2019 | Prelims | No-confidence motion can be moved only in? | Motions | Lok Sabha only |

### 22.11. MIND MAP

```text
PARLIAMENT (ARTS. 79-122)
├── COMPOSITION
│   ├── President + Rajya Sabha + Lok Sabha
│   ├── Rajya Sabha permanent (250 max), RS represents States
│   └── Lok Sabha directly elected (552 max), dissolvable
├── ELECTIONS
│   ├── LS: FPTP by constituencies
│   └── RS: PR by single transferable vote by MLAs
├── OFFICERS
│   ├── LS: Speaker, Deputy Speaker
│   └── RS: Chairman (VP), Deputy Chairman
├── LEGISLATION
│   ├── Money Bill (Art. 110) - LS exclusive, RS limited
│   ├── Ordinary Bill - either House
│   └── Joint sitting (Art. 108) for deadlock on Ordinary Bill
└── CONTROL DEVICES
    ├── Questions, Motions, Committees
    └── No-confidence motion only in Lok Sabha
```

